on" "value" "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nWe need to compute $U(f,P).$ \n\nNotice that for all subregions $R_{ij},$ we have that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\nM_{ij} = f(x_{i-1}, y_{j-1}) = 12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1}\n$$\n\nand the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080762/example-9497.json" "file_sha256" "4c89a56dcf20683dd925d89d3bb6bf7d801fb13257806dddeedcdcb6e4d0ff82"} {"question" "e-6122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [-1,4]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080762/example-6122.json" "file_sha256" "371bbde129813f5703476ca6214c58788c091b4a17521cb5b9d841a60651c10b"} {"question" "e-6122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since $m_{ij}=M_{ij}=3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}3\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =3({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =3(4-(-1))(2-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=15 & \\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 15=U(f,P),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 15.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080762/example-6122.json" "file_sha256" "371bbde129813f5703476ca6214c58788c091b4a17521cb5b9d841a60651c10b"} {"question" "e-9494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\\, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute $\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = (x_m^2 - x_0^2)(y_n-y_0)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080762/example-9494.json" "file_sha256" "819ed8bf0f3acc21e4c8174fc5cabdfa5e1e7e9cc7c012ed6753f22f70d4367c"} {"question" "e-9494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that $\\bar{x}_i = \\dfrac 1 2 (x_{i-1}+x_i)$ will always lie between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i,$ and similarly $\\bar{y}_j = \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1}+y_j)$ will always lie between $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1} + y_j) \\leq x_{i} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j},\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + 2y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\leq x_{i} + 2y_{j}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j$ and applying the double summation gives\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\right]\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P).\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(y_{j-1} + y_j\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P).\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})(3-1)=4 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({3}^{2}-{1}^{2})(2-0)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 4+16\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 20\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 20.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080762/example-9494.json" "file_sha256" "819ed8bf0f3acc21e4c8174fc5cabdfa5e1e7e9cc7c012ed6753f22f70d4367c"}] "usage" {"e-9494" [] "q-72627" [] "e-6122" [] "q-123489" [] "e-9497" []} "basis" 2833 "retractions" [[17592186129357 :question/problem "Let $R = [0, 2] \\cdot [1, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x + 2y$. Further, let\n\n$I = ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R$, we have\n$L(f, P) \\le I \\le U(f, P)$,\nwhere $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums, and\n\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$.\n\nWhat expression is missing from the double summation above?"] [17592186129790 :question/problem "Let $R = [0, 4] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y)$ be a function defined over $R$.\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$L(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(4x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j},; U(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(4x_{i} + 2y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$.\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f, P)$ and $U(f, P)$, compute $∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n$∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + x_{i})Δx_{i}Δy_{j} = (x_{m}^{2} - x_{0}^{2})(y_{n} - y_{0})$"] [17592186128975 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,1]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$. Further, let\n\n$\\displaystyle I = \\iint\\limits_{R} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm dA$\n\nbe the unique number such that over all rectangular partitions $P$ of $R$, we have\n$L(f,P)\\:\\leq I \\:\\leq U(f,P)$,\nwhere $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$ are the corresponding lower and upper Riemann sums. Write down the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P)$."] [17592186128975 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y)$, the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R$, where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)m_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j} \\\\\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe need to compute $U(f,P)$.\n\nNotice that for all subregions $R_{ij}$, we have that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 12-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. Therefore,\n\n$$\nM_{ij} = f(x_{i-1}, y_{j-1}) = 12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1}\n$$\n\nand the upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (12-x_{i-1}-y_{j-1})\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\n$$"] [17592186128929 :question/problem "Let $R = [-1,4]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A$,\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that $m_{ij} = M_{ij} = 3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R$, and $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion $R_{ij}$."] [17592186128929 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y)$, the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R$, where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)m_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j} \\\\\nU(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)M_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $m_{ij}$ and $M_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nm_{ij}=M_{ij}=3\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) = U(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)3Δx_{i}Δy_{j} \\\\\n&= 3(∑_(i = 1)^(m)Δx_{i})(∑_(j = 1)^(n)Δy_{j}) \\\\\n&= 3(4 - (-1))(2 - 1) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f, P) = 15 \\mid \\le ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA \\le 15 = U(f, P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 15\n$$"] [17592186128974 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R$.\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n$L(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j},; U(f, P)|= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i} + 2y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}$.\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P)$, compute $\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n$\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = (x_m^2 - x_0^2)(y_n-y_0)$"] [17592186128974 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x,y)$, the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq I \\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\bar{x}_i = \\dfrac 1 2 (x_{i-1}+x_i)\n$$\n\nwill always lie between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$, and similarly\n\n$$\n\\bar{y}_j = \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1}+y_j)\n$$\n\nwill always lie between $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 (y_{j-1} + y_j) \\leq x_{i} + {\\color{blue}2}y_{j}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nx_{i-1} + 2y_{j-1} \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\leq x_{i} + 2y_{j}\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j$ and applying the double summation gives\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}(x_{i-1} + x_i) + (y_{j-1} + y_j)\\right]\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(y_{j-1} + y_j\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j\\leq U(f,P)\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + x_{i})Δx_{i}Δy_{j} &= \\frac{1}{2}(2^{2} - 0^{2})(3 - 1) = 4 \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(y_{j - 1} + y_{j})Δx_{i}Δy_{j} &= (3^{2} - 1^{2})(2 - 0) = 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\nL(f, P) \\le 4 + 16 \\le U(f, P)⟹L(f, P) \\le 20 \\le U(f, P)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A = 20\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186125949 17592186125950 17592186125951 17592186125952 17592186125953 17592186128535 17592186128536 17592186128537 17592186128538 17592186128539] "immutable_fields" [[17592186125948 :answer-field/correct] [17592186125948 :answer-field/id] [17592